// === Porkchop plot (H-1) config — GRA-150 / bundle D ===
// Replaces the Efficient/Moderate/Fast placeholder in the transfer
// planner. A porkchop is a contour plot of total ΔV (or C3 /
// departure C3) over the (departure epoch, time-of-flight) plane;
// the player picks a cell instead of a fake "Fast" label on a
// Hohmann arc. The selected cell is converted to a real Lambert
// conic via `build_planned_transfer` so the arc and the ΔV agree
// for the first time.
//
// Schema lives entirely in this file: the planner reads
// `PorkchopConfig` once at Startup, derives per-transfer grids
// from the defaults + the first matching per-category override,
// and the UI reads back the same `PorkchopConfig` to render the
// grid + colormap + contours. Modders can:
//
//   * swap the colormap (add/remove/reorder `colormap` stops);
//   * change default resolution (`defaults.resolution_*`);
//   * add a per-category override with a new `match_key`
//     (e.g. "titan_approach" for a moon-of-Saturn special case);
//   * adjust bounds (`t_dep_window_days`, `tof_*` factors and
//     floors/ceilings) without recompiling Rust.
//
// The Coder wires this file into `PorkchopConfig` (struct) +
// `PorkchopGridDefaults` + `PorkchopCategoryOverride` +
// `PorkchopColorStop` in `src/fleets/components.rs` and registers
// the resource in `DataLoaderPlugin` (GRA-150 PR-D).
//
// Loader rules (enforced by load-time validation in
// tests/porkchop_config_data_tests.rs, added as part of bundle D):
//   1. `defaults.resolution_t_dep * defaults.resolution_tof`
//      must be ≤ 20000 (raised 12000 → 20000 alongside the
//      SUPERSAMPLE bump; ~6 s worst-case Lambert solve at the
//      LGD's 0.3 ms/cell budget, still inside the 3-day
//      staleness budget at 1 yr/s sim speed).
//   2. `colormap` must contain ≥ 2 stops; the first stop's
//      ΔV must be `0.0`. (Phase C: the previous "+∞" sentinel
//      stop requirement is gone — infeasible cells use the
//      dedicated `INFEASIBLE_COLOR` in `porkchop_color_ramp.rs`,
//      not a colormap stop.)
//   3. `category_overrides` are matched top-down, first hit wins;
//      a single override must be self-consistent (resolution ×
//      resolution ≤ 20000, tof_min_factor > 0, tof_min < tof_max).
//   4. The match keys used in code (`interplanetary`, `moon`,
//      `star_approach`, `interstellar`, `binary`, `flyby`,
//      `gravity_assist`, `course_correction`, `lagrange`,
//      `dwarf_planet`, `asteroid`, `comet`) are an *open* set —
//      unknown keys are ignored and fall through to `defaults`.
//      (`gravity_assist` added in GRA-367-RON for the P2 flyby
//      palette; the planner's `classify_body_transfer_category`
//      emits it once GRA-367-D wires the assist branch.
//      `short_hop` added in GRA-367-RON (operator-amended r2) for
//      the configurable short-hop bar; its `short_hop_options`
//      field was added to `PorkchopCategoryOverride` at the same
//      time, with `#[serde(default)]` only so older RON files
//      keep deserialising.  Rebase resolution (PR #229 onto main
//      after PR #230): the r2 amend's `Option<u32>` +
//      `skip_serializing_if` form was dropped in favour of main's
//      `Option<usize>` + `#[serde(default)]` form, which matches
//      PR #230's `build_short_hop_grid` consumer and the LGD's
//      r3 amend (`265f46b`); the field's wire semantics are
//      unchanged.)
//
// v0.5.0 ships with the 5 category_overrides below; the LGD
// authored defaults to be conservative (60-day window, 90×90
// resolution, ≤ 8 km/s tof baseline) so the first PR lands
// without performance surprises.
(
    defaults: (
        // ±60 days around the next optimal Hohmann window. Picked to
        // span the typical Earth-Mars synodic period (780 d) over a
        // ~month-and-a-half search range — small enough for a 1.2k
        // cell grid to complete in <400 ms, wide enough that the
        // cheapest cell is always within the visible grid.
        t_dep_window_days: 60.0,
        // TOF: 0.4× → 5.0× Hohmann time.  The 0.4 floor prevents the
        // player from picking a sub-orbital "transfer" that
        // Lambert's solver would reject.  The 5.0 ceiling (raised
        // from 2.5 in the v0.5.0 follow-up) was extended because
        // the old cap routinely truncated the cheap-transfer basin
        // for outer-planet transfers — a Hohmann-optimal Earth→Mars
        // window sits at ≈1.0× Hohmann time (258 d), but several
        // lower-ΔV long-arc options (bi-elliptic with an apoapsis
        // boost at Mars orbit, slow Hohmann-like transfers that
        // catch a more favourable synodic phase) live out at 2–4×
        // Hohmann time.  Without the wider cap the planner showed
        // the red top of the basin and the player concluded
        // "nothing useful above TOF = 200 d" when the real options
        // were sitting just past the visible boundary.  5.0× is
        // still bounded by `tof_ceiling_years = 10` (~ 14.25 yr
        // for Earth→Jupiter) so the grid stays useful for fast
        // rebuilds.
        tof_min_hohmann_factor: 0.4,
        tof_max_hohmann_factor: 5.0,
        tof_floor_days: 5.0,
        tof_ceiling_years: 10.0,
        // Resolution: 90 cols (t_dep) × 90 rows (tof) = 8100 cells
        // (was 70 × 70 = 4900; originally 40 × 50 = 2000). Bumped
        // from 40 × 50 because the wider 5.0× TOF range and the
        // per-column ΔV resolution were both under-sampled for
        // long-distance transfers, then bumped from 60 × 60
        // because direct Earth→Mercury / inner-planet porkchops
        // still showed 1-cell-wide color stripes in the cheap
        // basin (≈ 8 d/row over a ~525 d TOF span), then bumped
        // from 70 × 70 because wide-window transfers (Mars-class,
        // 780 d synodic) still read as blocky ΔV bands — the
        // 5000-cell cap was squeezing the row count down to
        // ~12 rows on a 525-day span.  With the adaptive Y-axis
        // trim (see `rendered_tof_bounds_s`) the populated region
        // of a Saturn-class porkchop spans ~30 rendered rows of
        // the 90 solved rows, so the per-cell TOF resolution on
        // the cheap-transfer basin is now ~5.8 d at Saturn (~17
        // d at 70 × 70).  8100 cells is well under the 20000-cell
        // budget enforced by validator rule #1, and the rebuild
        // cost at ~0.3 ms/cell is ~2.4 s worst case — still inside
        // the 3-day staleness window so the deferred build path
        // doesn't visibly lag at 1 yr/s sim speed.
        //
        // The RON resolution values are MINIMUMS, not absolutes.
        // `build_porkchop_grid_with_params` runs them through
        // `adaptive_resolution`, which bumps `cols` / `rows` up
        // if the natural `(t_dep_window, tof_span)` is wider than
        // the static values can comfortably sample (target:
        // 2-day t_dep step, 3-day TOF step — see
        // `MAX_T_DEP_STEP_DAYS` and `MAX_TOF_STEP_DAYS` in
        // `src/fleets/porkchop.rs`).  Total cells are clamped at
        // 20000 by shrinking whichever dimension has the looser
        // step first.  This is the structural fix for
        // Earth→Mercury via Venus GA, where the Earth↔Venus
        // synodic window (~614 d) demands finer t_dep sampling
        // than any static column count could provide without
        // inflating the Saturn-class grid unnecessarily.
        resolution_t_dep: 90,
        resolution_tof: 90,
        // C3 ceiling (km²/s²) above which a cell is rendered as
        // red "infeasible" and is not selectable. Picked to
        // match the helios theme's RED palette transition; the
        // planner will surface a "widen search?" prompt when
        // *no* cell in the window is feasible.
        c3_ceiling_km2_s2: 400.0,
    ),

    // Per-category overrides. Matched top-down, first hit wins.
    // A body's transfer category is derived from the (origin,
    // destination) pair in the planner; the Coder's
    // `classify_transfer_category` returns the match key.
    //
    // Every override MUST define the full `PorkchopCategoryOverride`
    // schema (see `src/fleets/components.rs`):
    //   t_dep_window_days, tof_min_hohmann_factor, tof_max_hohmann_factor,
    //   tof_floor_days, tof_ceiling_years, resolution_t_dep, resolution_tof,
    //   c3_ceiling_km2_s2.
    // None of these fields carry `#[serde(default)]`, so a missing
    // value aborts deserialisation and the loader falls back to
    // `PorkchopConfig::default()` at runtime — silent loss of every
    // category override.  `tests/data::porkchop_config_ron_loads_cleanly`
    // is the strict CI gate that catches this regression.
    category_overrides: [
        // Interstellar: blow out the grid so the player can see
        // the geological-timescale basin. The 500-yr cap from
        // MAX_CURVED_CROSS_STAR_TRANSFER_TIME_S still applies at
        // runtime; here we set the 100-yr ceiling and the UI
        // flags cells > 50 yr in red.
        (
            match_key: "interstellar",
            t_dep_window_days: 730.0,           // 2 yr
            tof_min_hohmann_factor: 0.2,
            tof_max_hohmann_factor: 5.0,
            tof_floor_days: 30.0,               // ≪ Hohmann time for any interstellar leg; floor is purely a sanity guard
            tof_ceiling_years: 100.0,
            resolution_t_dep: 70,
            resolution_tof: 60,
            c3_ceiling_km2_s2: 400.0,
        ),
        // Lagrange-point transfers (GRA-NNN): the L1–L5 destination
        // is a synthetic heliocentric orbit co-rotating with the
        // planet (and phase-shifted for L3/L4/L5).  The 30-day
        // window covers a synodic-period slice; the 1.5–3× Hohmann
        // TOF envelope matches patched-conic Hohmanns between the
        // parking orbit and the LP.  Low resolution (50×40 = 2000
        // cells) keeps the build cheap enough to run synchronously
        // on every planner tick — the GRA-NNN Lagrange grid is
        // rendered in the same frame the player picks the LP.
        // Ceiling 0.5 yr covers L1/L2 short falls and L3/L4/L5
        // Hohmann-style phasing windows comfortably.
        (
            match_key: "lagrange",
            t_dep_window_days: 30.0,
            tof_min_hohmann_factor: 0.5,
            tof_max_hohmann_factor: 3.0,
            tof_floor_days: 1.0,
            tof_ceiling_years: 0.5,
            resolution_t_dep: 50,
            resolution_tof: 40,
            c3_ceiling_km2_s2: 400.0,
        ),
        // Moon transfers: tight window, fine grid. Short TOFs and
        // small distances → cheap Lambert solves, can afford more
        // cells. The 14-day window is roughly one lunar orbital
        // period; the player can always pan to a new window if the
        // cheapest cell is off-grid. Bumped from 50×40 to 70×50
        // (3500 cells) so the cheap-transfer basin is sampled finely
        // enough to land near the optimal Hohmann phase on every
        // build. Below the 20000-cell ceiling.
        (
            match_key: "moon",
            t_dep_window_days: 14.0,
            tof_min_hohmann_factor: 0.5,
            tof_max_hohmann_factor: 1.8,
            tof_floor_days: 0.5,
            tof_ceiling_years: 0.165,           // ≈ 60 days (was mis-named `tof_ceiling_days`; serde silently ignored it)
            resolution_t_dep: 70,
            resolution_tof: 50,
            c3_ceiling_km2_s2: 400.0,
        ),
        // Star approach (any body → star): the round-trip
        // Hohmann equivalent is years, so a wide window and a
        // generous TOF range are warranted. The 365-day window
        // is the synodic period scale for a typical body-star
        // configuration; the 5-yr ceiling catches realistic
        // solar Oberth transfers. Bumped from 50×40 to 60×50
        // (3000 cells) for higher basin resolution.
        (
            match_key: "star_approach",
            t_dep_window_days: 365.0,
            tof_min_hohmann_factor: 0.3,
            tof_max_hohmann_factor: 3.0,
            tof_floor_days: 10.0,               // ≈ Oberth window: anything shorter is a sub-orbital burn
            tof_ceiling_years: 5.0,
            resolution_t_dep: 60,
            resolution_tof: 50,
            c3_ceiling_km2_s2: 400.0,
        ),
        // Short-hop (GRA-367 P3, operator-amended r2 — see
        // `docs/design/TRANSFER_PLANNER_HARMONISATION.md`):
        // replaces the legacy 3-card Efficient/Moderate/Fast row
        // with a configurable N-row porkchop bar (default 5 in this
        // entry; LGD may retune without a code change). The
        // preset spread is `{0.6×, 0.85×, 1.0×, 1.4×, 2.0×}` of
        // Hohmann time, so the player sees *variety* of options
        // rather than three copies of the same transfer.
        // `short_hop_options` powers `build_short_hop_grid`'s row
        // count (clamped 3..=9 at the call site; `None` falls back
        // to the legacy 3 behaviour). Resolution fields drive the
        // single-column per-row Δv sweep used to colour the bar.
        (
            match_key: "short_hop",
            t_dep_window_days: 14.0,
            tof_min_hohmann_factor: 0.6,
            tof_max_hohmann_factor: 2.0,
            tof_floor_days: 0.5,
            tof_ceiling_years: 0.165,
            resolution_t_dep: 1,
            resolution_tof: 50,
            c3_ceiling_km2_s2: 400.0,
            short_hop_options: Some(25),
            // GRA-385 (synodic sweep): 7 launch-time columns over
            // the 14-day dep window (= 2-day step).  Catches the
            // synodic-phase sweep on Galilean / Saturnian moons
            // without flooding the player with redundant cells.
            short_hop_t_dep_steps: Some(12),
        ),
        // Gravity assist (GRA-367 P2 / GRA-386 bump / follow-up):
        // the planner re-solves a selected cell with
        // `compute_gravity_assist_for_cell` once the player picks a
        // flyby body. The (t_dep, TOF) sweep is tighter than the
        // interplanetary default because the assist's window-period
        // constrains useful launches to a ~2-month band per assist
        // candidate.
        //
        // GRA-386: bumped the resolution from 20×15 = 300 cells to
        // 50×40 = 2000 cells (still well under the 20000-cell
        // ceiling). The previous 20×15 sizing was for the legacy
        // non-clickable sub-grid; now that the GA grid IS the panel
        // (GRA-385 view-mode toggle), 300 cells gives only a ~3-day
        // column step on a 60-day window — far too coarse to pick a
        // savings band of ~0.05 km/s.
        //
        // Follow-up bump: 50×40 → 80×60 = 4800 cells. The 50-col
        // setting sampled a ~614-day Earth↔Venus-synodic window at
        // ~12.3 d/column, which was wider than the cheap-assist
        // basin (~10–15 d) and rendered the basin as 1-cell-wide
        // color stripes on Earth→Mercury via Venus (the cheapest
        // assist was effectively invisible to the player). 80 cols
        // over the same window gives ~7.7 d/column, and the
        // builder's `adaptive_resolution` (see defaults block
        // above) can push cols higher — up to ~94 — while still
        // keeping the row step at the 5-day target, so the basin
        // spans 1.5–2.5 cells instead of 0.8. Rebuild cost ~1.44 s
        // worst case (4800 cells × 0.3 ms); the 3-day staleness
        // budget at 1 yr/s sim speed is still respected.
        //
        // Resolution bump: 80×60 → 90×90 = 8100 cells (under the
        // 20000-cell ceiling). 90 cols over the same 614-day
        // synodic window gives 6.8 d/column at the floor, and
        // `adaptive_resolution` pushes cols to ~307 (2.0 d/col)
        // for the wide-window case while keeping rows at the 3-day
        // step target. The basin now spans 1.5–2.5 cells of the
        // cheapest color band on the GA plot as well as the
        // interplanetary one. Rebuild cost ~2.4 s worst case
        // (8100 cells × 0.3 ms); still inside the 3-day staleness
        // budget.
        (
            match_key: "gravity_assist",
            t_dep_window_days: 60.0,
            tof_min_hohmann_factor: 0.4,
            tof_max_hohmann_factor: 2.5,
            tof_floor_days: 5.0,
            tof_ceiling_years: 3.0,
            resolution_t_dep: 90,
            resolution_tof: 90,
            c3_ceiling_km2_s2: 400.0,
        ),
    ],

    // ΔV (km/s) → RGBA stops. Linear interpolation between adjacent
    // stops. RGBA tuples are (R, G, B, A) with components in 0..=255.
    //
    // Phase C: the previous "+∞" sentinel stop has been removed.
    // Infeasible cells (Lambert solver failed) are now rendered
    // with the dedicated `INFEASIBLE_COLOR` (dark grey) in
    // `src/ui/porkchop_color_ramp.rs` and the baked texture in
    // `src/ui/porkchop_panel.rs` — they bypass the colormap
    // entirely. The runtime colormap covers only the feasible
    // ΔV range, simplifying the validator and removing the
    // `delta_v_km_s: inf` literal that previously failed to
    // parse with old Rust syntax.
    //
    // Stops are tuned to match the helios theme palette:
    //   GREEN  = best in class (≤ 4 km/s)
    //   YELLOW = competitive  (4–8 km/s)
    //   AMBER  = expensive    (8–15 km/s)
    //   RED    = prohibitive  (> 15 km/s)
    colormap: [
        (delta_v_km_s: 0.0,                    rgba: ( 40, 200,  80, 220)),
        (delta_v_km_s: 4.0,                    rgba: (220, 200,  60, 220)),
        (delta_v_km_s: 8.0,                    rgba: (230, 140,  40, 220)),
        (delta_v_km_s: 15.0,                   rgba: (220,  60,  60, 220)),
    ],
    // Iso-ΔV contour lines drawn on top of the grid, in km/s.
    // The minimum (cheapest cell) contour is always drawn; the
    // maximum is suppressed when the cell is at the edge of the
    // grid (would otherwise draw a half-line at the boundary).
    contour_levels_km_s: [3.0, 5.0, 8.0, 12.0],
    // Cells with total ΔV above this (km/s) are rendered as
    // "off the chart" — the colour is the +∞ stop and a hatch
    // pattern is overlaid. Set to a value safely above the
    // RED stop (15 km/s) so the four colour bands are all
    // visible inside the grid.
    display_max_dv_km_s: 20.0,
)
